<article class="env-lemma" id="03CA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03CA">Lemma <span data-tag="03CA">10.87.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $0 \to K_ i \to L_ i \to M_ i \to 0$ be short exact sequences of $R$-modules, $i \geq 1$ which fit into maps of short exact sequences </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ 0 \ar[r] &  K_ i \ar[r] &  L_ i \ar[r] &  M_ i \ar[r] &  0 \\  0 \ar[r] &  K_{i + 1} \ar[r] \ar[u] &  L_{i + 1} \ar[r] \ar[u] &  M_{i + 1} \ar[r] \ar[u] &  0}  \]
</div>
  <p> If for every $i$ there exists a $c = c(i) \geq i$ such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(K_ c \to K_ i) = \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(K_ j \to K_ i)$ for all $j \geq c$, then the sequence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K_ i \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits L_ i \to \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits M_ i \to 0  \]
</div>
  <p> is exact. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is a special case of the more general Lemma <a href="/tag/0598" data-tag="0598">10.86.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>